'''ape''' (āp), ''n.'' [< ME. ''ape'', < AS. ''apa'' = Fries. ''apa'' = D. ''aap'' (pl. ''apen'') = LG. ''ape'' = OHG. ''affo'', MHG. ''affe'', G. ''affe'' = Icel. ''api'' = Sw. ''apa'' = Dan. ''abe'', ape; not a native Teut. word, but prob. (like Ir. Gael. ''ap'', ''apa'', W. ''ab'', ''epa'', OBohem. ''op'', mod. Bohem. ''opice'', Sloven. ''opica'', Upper Sorbian ''vopica'', ORuss. ''opica'', Russ. ''obeziyana'') borrowed in very early times (appar. with loss of orig. initial ''k'') from the East; cf. Gr. κῆπος, also κῆβος, κεῖπος (see ''Cebus''), Skt. ''kapi'', ape. The Skt. name is usually referred to Skt. √ *''kap'', ''kamp'', tremble.] '''1.''' A monkey; a quadrumanous animal; some animal of the old order ''Quadrumana''; a member of one of the modern families ''Simiidæ'', ''Cynopithecidæ'', and ''Cebidæ'', especially one which attracts attention by mimicking man.—'''2.''' More specifically, a tailless monkey; a monkey with a very short tail; a magot, macaque, or pig-tailed baboon: as, the Barbary ''ape'' (''Inuus ecaudatus''); the Celebes black ''ape'' (''Cynopithecus niger'').—'''3.''' Technically, a man-like monkey; a simian proper, or a member of the modern family ''Simiidæ'', forming a kind of connecting link between man and the lower animals, and hence termed ''anthropoid'' (which see). {{smaller|These apes are catarrhine simians without cheek-pouches or developed tail, and having a dental formula identical with that of man. The species are few, being only the gorilla, chimpanzee, orangs, and gibbons.}} '''4.''' An imitator; a mimic.
{{smaller block|O sleep, thou ''ape'' of death. {{right|''Shak.'', Cymbeline, ii. 2.}}}}

